WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.729
2021 was the year of coups.

00:00:03.729 --> 00:00:10.730
In total, six countries experienced at least
one attempt — five of which were successful.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:17.570
That’s as many as the previous six years
— from 2015 to 20 — put together and over

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.230
three times the two-decade average.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:26.320
The last time the world saw this many in a
single calendar year, in fact, was after the

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.480
fall of the Soviet Union in 1991.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.860
The UN Secretary General went so far as to
call it an “epidemic”.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.300
Clearly, coups are not a thing of the past.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.940
But what’s remarkable is how little they’ve
changed.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:50.850
In 1968, political scientist Edward Luttwak
released “Coup d'État: A Practical Handbook”

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.960
— essentially a step-by-step cookbook for
would-be dictators.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:01.180
Today, over 50 years later, the same basic
recipe is used to seize control of countries

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.170
like Mali, Guinea, Sudan, and Myanmar.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:09.070
These dictators even look like they did in
the 60s.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:16.610
So, how do coups work, why do these steps
seem to transcend time and place, and what

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.460
does that tell us about how to prevent them?

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.409
Sponsored by Storyblocks, where you can find
over a million high quality stock videos for

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:28.710
all your creative projects.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.710
To answer our questions, we’re going to
have to temporarily inhabit the mind of an

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:36.750
aspiring, Machiavvelian autocrat.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.619
Our very first step is to select a target.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:46.740
As Americans learned on January 6th, no country
is immune from coups and it always pays to

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.130
stay vigilant against them.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:55.660
Still, there are some characteristics that
make a coup more or less likely to succeed…

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.650
We see that in their geographic distribution.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:03.490
Coups are not as common as this chart from
earlier might suggest.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:09.440
Rather, a few countries seem to be trapped
in a perpetual cycle.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.469
Of the countries which have seen at least
one attempt since 1950, the average number

00:02:14.469 --> 00:02:19.540
during that period was five — or once every
fourteen years.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:26.209
They’ve been a regular feature of Thai politics,
for instance, since 1912.

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:29.249
And this is just a list of the successful
ones.

00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:34.219
If you include failed attempts, there’ve
been at least 30.

00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:39.780
The Sahel region of Africa, likewise, has
been referred to as a “coup belt”.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:47.349
Since 1950, Sudan has experienced 16, and
both Sierra Leone and Ghana, 10.

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:52.730
Many of these countries are poor, yes, but
not all poor countries are represented here.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:57.279
So what do these, in particular, have in common?

00:02:57.279 --> 00:03:02.689
For starters, they tend to have a high concentration
of power.

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:06.879
As we’ll see, coups rarely command a numerical
advantage.

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:13.010
They’re surgical operations, not overwhelming
displays of firepower.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:18.610
Our ideal country has a definite political
center — a single Capital city home to all

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.790
the administrative and bureaucratic headquarters.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:25.919
AKA, a single point of failure.

00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:30.359
The greater the separation of powers, the
more branches of government we’ll have to

00:03:30.359 --> 00:03:31.949
infiltrate.

00:03:31.949 --> 00:03:35.329
The same is true of redundancy in general.

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:42.739
Syria’s Assad, for example, maintained not
2, 3, or even 4, but five separate intelligence

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:44.169
services.

00:03:44.169 --> 00:03:48.379
One might defect, but it’s very unlikely
that all four will.

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:51.849
Besides, someone has to spy on the spies.

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:57.569
Next, the ideal target also has low political
participation.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:04.579
Where revolutions are built on passion, coups,
on the other hand, thrive on apathy.

00:04:04.579 --> 00:04:09.279
Dissatisfaction with the current government,
of course, is preferable to satisfaction.

00:04:09.279 --> 00:04:12.729
But the more important dimension is engagement.

00:04:12.729 --> 00:04:19.040
Though we’ll probably want to package our
actions with some ideological window dressing,

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.150
remember: we, the elite, are performing the
coup, not the masses.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.000
We’re not soliciting rebellion.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.570
Quite the contrary.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.940
We want to avoid civilian intervention.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:37.570
The population should know very little about
politics and — above all — believe their

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:40.180
involvement simply doesn’t matter.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:44.530
“They’re all the same, anyway” and “Who
am I to change things?”

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:48.560
are music to our ears.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:54.820
Now that we’ve chosen a target, it’s time
to move on to step two: Infiltrate.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:05:00.940
This crucial phase will almost entirely determine
our ultimate success or failure.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:06.250
But set down your aviators and take off your
barret, because this is largely an academic

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:07.270
exercise.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:13.630
Our job is to draw one, giant org chart of
the current government.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:19.040
This includes everyone in the bureaucracy,
military, and party — from the Prime Minister

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.950
all the way down to his limo driver.

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:28.190
Except, unlike one you’d find on the national
website or Wikipedia, ours will include only

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.690
the real sources of power.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:35.430
Corruption and simple mismanagement are likely
to have produced a large number of pencil

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:40.300
pushers and ceremonial leaders who we can
safely ignore.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.940
We can cross off, for example, the 80-year
old general who everyone knows is all but

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:46.930
retired.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:51.560
Now what we’re left with is effectively
a map of power.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:58.580
Our next task is to divide everyone into one
of three categories: Allies, indifferent pragmatists,

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.490
and enemies.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:07.360
To do this, we’ll start by asking: Do we
absolutely need this person’s active support?

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:12.310
If the answer is yes — whether because they’re
a top decision maker or simply know the email

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:17.220
passwords — our only option is to buy their
support.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:21.540
If there are multiple candidates for a given
role, we’ll pick the less qualified among

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:22.810
them.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:26.200
We want someone who feels under-utilized.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:29.820
Someone who's been passed over for promotions.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.650
With less to lose, they’ll more readily
join our cause, where we’ve promised them

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.930
a shiny new office.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:42.050
Some, of course, will be tempted to alert
their authorities of our plan.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:45.640
Luckily, we anticipate this and raise the
stakes.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:50.430
“Your superiors are already on board,”
we tell them.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:54.000
Joining us should feel like the ‘safe’
option.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.580
We’ve now established our small circle of
allies.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:04.570
But that still leaves a wide cast of characters,
ranging from powerful military commanders

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:07.280
to glorified mall cops.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:14.270
To identify the real threats, we’ll simply
ask: Is this person loyal to the ruling party?

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:18.350
Our enemies are those for whom the answer
is ‘yes’.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:21.100
Many will be loud but powerless figureheads.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:25.780
They may even incidentally help our cause
thanks to their own unpopularity.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:30.710
The others we’ll make sure are distracted
at the time of the coup.

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:36.550
Off fighting some distant war, for example,
or we may even create for them a wild goose

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:38.590
chase.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:44.350
Everyone else, which constitutes the vast
majority, are “indifferent pragmatists”.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:49.540
Not loyal to any particular party or ideology,
they’ll sit back in silence while the coup

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:53.810
unfolds, waiting to see how things play out.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:59.419
No need to worry about them; we’ll slowly
win their support as we consolidate power.

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:04.090
…Which brings us to step three: freeze.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:09.900
If we’ve carried out the first two steps
well, this phase will seem shockingly easy.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:15.400
With our allies in place and awaiting instructions,
one could argue the coup has already occurred

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:17.400
— it’s just that nobody knows it yet.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.720
This, again, is what differentiates a coup.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:27.260
Although it’s often performed by the military,
it is not, primarily a military operation.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:31.810
Ideally, in fact, no one will be killed or
even hurt.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:36.300
It’s best to avoid creating a martyr or
risk breeding discontent.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:41.099
Besides, we have neither an advantage in strength
nor numbers.

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:48.959
We’ll begin this phase by freezing all movement
— both that of people and information.

00:08:48.959 --> 00:08:54.809
Airports will be shut down, TV stations taken
off the air, roads blocked, and the internet

00:08:54.809 --> 00:08:56.529
cut.

00:08:56.529 --> 00:09:01.420
Lack of information will make civilian resistance
difficult — the average person won’t even

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.450
know what’s going on.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:08.130
The disorder will be indistinguishable from,
say, a natural disaster.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:14.639
Next, while our allies form a perimeter around
the Capital, a small tactical team will covertly

00:09:14.639 --> 00:09:18.589
enter the palace and arrest the current government.

00:09:18.589 --> 00:09:24.009
Upon noticing the turmoil, whatever army,
police, and security forces are outside the

00:09:24.009 --> 00:09:27.250
perimeter will make their way to it.

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:32.019
But our secret weapon, remember, is chaos
and confusion.

00:09:32.019 --> 00:09:37.290
At the perimeter, they’ll discover not hostile
foreign targets but calm soldiers wearing

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:44.300
their exact same uniforms . 9 times out of
10, Occam’s Razor will prevail.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:49.740
When faced with uncertainty, the vast majority
of soldiers will do as they’re trained:

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:54.220
await further instructions, assuming incompetence.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:10:00.269
Even if they suspect something is fishy, few,
if any, will risk their lives on a hunch.

00:10:00.269 --> 00:10:04.779
Once again, apathy will play in our favor.

00:10:04.779 --> 00:10:10.110
By now, the opposition has been silenced,
the public thoroughly confused, and the physical

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:13.259
symbols of power occupied.

00:10:13.259 --> 00:10:16.680
It may seem as though the coup is complete.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.930
But note the word “symbols”.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:24.619
Power is not merely the possession of the
telephone on the Resolute Desk, but the odds

00:10:24.619 --> 00:10:29.209
that the person on the other end will follow
the orders spoken into it.

00:10:29.209 --> 00:10:34.339
We may posess the telephone, at this point,
but one could say there effectively is no

00:10:34.339 --> 00:10:35.399
government.

00:10:35.399 --> 00:10:43.089
Our task is to somehow transform this symbolic
power into actual power.

00:10:43.089 --> 00:10:46.399
What we’re attempting is a sleight of hand
trick.

00:10:46.399 --> 00:10:52.809
We begin by deceiving just one person — convincing
them that we already have power.

00:10:52.809 --> 00:10:58.149
Their belief in our power thus becomes our
foothold — something tangible we can point

00:10:58.149 --> 00:11:00.160
to as evidence of our authority.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:02.720
It’s a self-fulfilling prophecy.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.999
A lie that — we hope — becomes true by
virtue of speaking it.

00:11:07.999 --> 00:11:10.889
It’s faking it until you make it.

00:11:10.889 --> 00:11:12.720
But be careful.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:17.130
This positive feedback loop can also work
against us.

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:22.329
Slow down or lay bare the seams of our rule
and resistance will develop.

00:11:22.329 --> 00:11:26.499
And as soon as one person resists, all others
will be emboldened.

00:11:26.499 --> 00:11:31.589
Speed, therefore, is of the utmost importance.

00:11:31.589 --> 00:11:38.809
With each building we control, flag we raise,
and law we pass, the greater the rebel-or-comply

00:11:38.809 --> 00:11:42.320
risk calculus will fall in our favor.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:47.230
In practical terms, this means, as soon as
possible, we’ll perform the ultimate coup

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:52.180
cliché — a victorious televised address.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:56.579
This is why the old government was illegitimate
and this is why we needed to intervene for

00:11:56.579 --> 00:12:01.089
the good of the nation, slash democracy, slash
economy.

00:12:01.089 --> 00:12:06.569
To increase our authoritative momentum, we’ll
immediately institute a dramatic change in

00:12:06.569 --> 00:12:12.069
policy — re-distribute land, kick out foreigners,
or do something else rash.

00:12:12.069 --> 00:12:17.470
Oh and by the way, in case you don’t see
things the same way, there’s no point resisting

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:21.809
anyway: the battle has already been fought
and won.

00:12:21.809 --> 00:12:23.339
Fill in the blanks.

00:12:23.339 --> 00:12:26.040
It’s Mad-Libs, really.

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:30.980
Much of the international community will no
doubt condemn our rule, but they will inevitably

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:35.130
recognize it as it becomes clear they have
no other choice.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:41.179
Now, although the active part of our coup
is now over, statistically speaking, the most

00:12:41.179 --> 00:12:44.529
dangerous phase has only now begun.

00:12:44.529 --> 00:12:49.930
Recall, from earlier, the chart of coups since
1950.

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:58.470
Of them, 242 were successful and a remarkably-even
244 were failures.

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:04.029
What this means is that if — and, granted,
this is a big if — your coup makes it past

00:13:04.029 --> 00:13:10.139
the planning stage and becomes public — it
has an almost exactly 50% chance of succeeding.

00:13:10.139 --> 00:13:17.470
Coups, as their similarity across time demonstrates,
aren’t really that difficult to pull off.

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:20.139
At least, in these countries.

00:13:20.139 --> 00:13:23.279
What is difficult is holding on to that power.

00:13:23.279 --> 00:13:28.949
That’s because, if you think about it, a
coup is both the product of a lack of legitimacy

00:13:28.949 --> 00:13:31.319
and a cause of it.

00:13:31.319 --> 00:13:35.470
Where they occur frequently, they become normalized.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:41.880
Each successful operation invigorates the
next, in a downward, bottomless spiral.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:46.649
The public slowly loses hope, believing none
of it matters.

00:13:46.649 --> 00:13:48.509
Apathy sets in.

00:13:48.509 --> 00:13:51.189
Apathy which makes the next one even easier.

00:13:51.189 --> 00:13:58.450
Thus, while it’s often not enough, resistance,
however small and however symbolic, still

00:13:58.450 --> 00:13:59.679
matters.

00:13:59.679 --> 00:14:04.179
Myanmar, for instance, continues to bravely
defy authority.

00:14:04.179 --> 00:14:09.139
Refusing, despite the country’s turbulent
and at times repetitive history, to succumb

00:14:09.139 --> 00:14:10.139
to numbness.

00:14:10.139 --> 00:14:15.519
And it’s through images like these that
we, thousands of miles away, connect with

00:14:15.519 --> 00:14:18.149
these important stories.

00:14:18.149 --> 00:14:22.689
Video transcends time, place, language, and
culture — it’s the most compelling way

00:14:22.689 --> 00:14:27.920
